---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **세계의 Top10 자리 중 한국 작품이 몇 %인가.** (`/world-share`)
 *
 * ── 왜 이 지면인가 ─────────────────────────────────────────────
 * 「한류가 커진다」는 말은 어디에나 있는데 **자리로 잰 수**는 아무 데도 없다.
 * 넷플릭스는 나라마다 주마다 딱 스무 자리를 낸다. 고정된 파이라 그대로 견줄 수 있다.
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **93개국을 1위부터 늘어놓지 않는다.** 그건 나라 자랑이지 우리가 낼 것이 아니다.
 *    띠로 묶고, 아홉 나라는 **순서가 아니라 「하나도 예외가 없다」**를 보이려고 낸다.
 * ⛔ 시청량이 아니다. 나라별 목록에는 시간이 없다.
 * ⛔ 까닭을 지어내지 않는다. 무엇이 줄었는지까지가 우리가 아는 것이다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 *
 * ── ⚠ 수를 여기 손으로 적지 않는다 ────────────────────────────
 * DESC 까지 자료에서 만든다. 손으로 적으면 자료가 바뀔 때 **적어 둔 것만 옛 수로 남는다.**
 * 그 자리를 지키는 자가 `scripts/check-world-share-article.mjs` 에 있다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-world-share.json';
import season from '../../data/wikitip-season.json';
/* ⛔ 197 을 손으로 박았다가 자에 걸렸다. 이 수는 여기서 읽는다 */
import reach from '../../data/wikitip-reach.json';

const TITLE = "How much of the world's Netflix top 10 is Korean";

const 아홉 = data.groups[1];
const 처음 = 아홉.byYear[0];
const 마지막 = 아홉.byYear[아홉.byYear.length - 1];
const 떨어진 = +(처음.pc - 마지막.pc).toFixed(1);
const 첫가름 = data.ninesDecomposed[0];
const 끝가름 = data.ninesDecomposed[data.ninesDecomposed.length - 1];

const DESC =
  `Korean titles hold ${data.worldPc}% of the top-10 places Netflix publishes across `
  + `${data.countryCount} countries. The share has not moved since ${data.byYear[0].year}, `
  + `but in the ${아홉.countries} Asian markets where it was highest it fell from ${처음.pc}% to ${마지막.pc}%.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: `${data.weekFrom}/${data.weekTo}`,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://query.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd} canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/world-share">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Netflix publishes twenty places per country per week — ten films, ten series. It is a fixed pie,
    which makes it one of the few things about the Korean wave that can be counted rather than
    asserted. We counted {data.countryCount} countries with a complete record across
    {' '}{data.weekCount} weeks, {data.weekFrom} to {data.weekTo}.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>{data.worldPc}% of the world, and half of Korea</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">All {data.countryCount} markets</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.worldPc}%</td>
            <td class="sub">{data.koreanSlots.toLocaleString('en-US')} of {data.totalSlots.toLocaleString('en-US')} places</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">The median country</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.medianCountryPc}%</td>
            <td class="sub">half the markets sit below this</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">{data.home.name} itself</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.home.byYear[data.home.byYear.length - 1].pc}%</td>
            <td class="sub">in {data.home.byYear[data.home.byYear.length - 1].year}</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Korea's own figure surprises people first.</b> Even at home, in the market where every Korean
      release lands and the audience needs no subtitles, fewer than half of Netflix's top-10 places
      hold a Korean title.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where the share is high, and where it is not</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Korean share</th><th>Markets</th><th>Which</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.bands.map((b) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{b.to === null ? `${b.from}% and above` : `${b.from}–${b.to}%`}</td>
              <td class="tot">{b.countries}</td>
              <td class="nm names">
                {b.names.length <= 10
                  ? b.names.join(', ')
                  : `${b.names.slice(0, 6).join(', ')} and ${b.names.length - 6} more`}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Every market in the top band is in East or Southeast Asia. The bottom band is northern Europe
      and the English-speaking world. <b>These are bands, not a league table</b> — we are not ranking
      {' '}{data.countryCount} countries against one another, and the order inside a band carries no
      meaning we would defend.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The global share has not moved</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Year</th><th>Weeks</th><th>Korean share</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.byYear.map((y) => (
            <tr><td class="nm">{y.year}</td><td>{y.weeks}</td><td class="tot">{y.pc.toFixed(1)}%</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Four complete years, and the share ends where it started. <b>Only complete calendar years are
      used here.</b> {data.partialYears.map((y) => `${y.year} has ${y.weeks} weeks`).join(' and ')},
      so they are reported separately rather than blended into a trend
      ({data.partialYears.map((y) => `${y.year}: ${y.pc}%`).join(', ')}).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What that average hides</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Group</th>
            {data.byYear.map((y) => (<th>{y.year}</th>))}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.groups.map((g) => (
            <tr class={g.countries === 9 ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{g.group}</td>
              {g.byYear.map((y) => (<td>{y.pc.toFixed(1)}%</td>))}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      The {아홉.countries} markets where Korean titles were strongest gave up <b>{떨어진} points in
      four years</b> — better than a third of the share they held. The English-speaking markets did
      not pick it up. The global average stayed flat because {아홉.countries} countries falling is a
      small thing inside an average of {data.countryCount}.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>All {data.ninesByCountry.length} fell. That is the finding, not the order.</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Market</th><th>{처음.year}</th><th>{마지막.year}</th><th>Change</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.ninesByCountry.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{c.name}</td>
              <td>{c.first}%</td><td>{c.last}%</td>
              <td class="tot">{c.change > 0 ? '+' : '−'}{Math.abs(c.change)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>There is no exception.</b> One market falling is a market. All of them falling, across a
      range of languages, incomes and neighbouring content industries, is the same thing happening
      to all of them. We print every one rather than the worst or the best.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Fewer titles, not shorter runs</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Year</th><th>Korean share</th><th>Korean titles charting</th>
            <th>Places per Korean title</th><th>All titles charting</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.ninesDecomposed.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{r.year}</td>
              <td>{r.pc.toFixed(1)}%</td>
              <td class="tot">{r.koreanTitles}</td>
              <td>{r.slotsPerKoreanTitle}</td>
              <td>{r.allTitles.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      A share can fall two ways: the same titles hold fewer places each, or fewer titles get in at
      all. <b>It is the second.</b> Distinct Korean titles fell from {첫가름.koreanTitles} to
      {' '}{끝가름.koreanTitles}, while what each one did once it arrived barely changed
      ({첫가름.slotsPerKoreanTitle} places to {끝가름.slotsPerKoreanTitle}). Meanwhile the total number
      of distinct titles charting rose from {첫가름.allTitles.toLocaleString('en-US')} to
      {' '}{끝가름.allTitles.toLocaleString('en-US')}.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The control: Korea's own chart</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      There is an obvious way this page could be wrong. We identify Korean titles through Wikidata,
      and Wikidata records a new title later than Netflix charts it. If our recognition were quietly
      decaying, recent years would look emptier of Korean content whether or not anything changed.{' '}
      <b> Korea's own chart tests that</b>, because it is measured with exactly the same
      identification — and it is the chart most densely packed with Korean titles, so a decaying
      ruler would shorten here first and hardest.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Market</th>{data.home.byYear.map((y) => (<th>{y.year}</th>))}</tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">{data.home.name}</td>
            {data.home.byYear.map((y) => (<td class="tot">{y.pc.toFixed(1)}%</td>))}
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      It does not sag. It ends within a tenth of a point of where it started while the nine markets
      lost more than ten points, so whatever moved was not the ruler.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The peaks are one title</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Week</th><th>Korean share</th><th>Korean places</th><th>Largest single title</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {[...data.peakWeeks.slice(0, 3), data.troughWeeks[0]].map((w) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{w.week}</td>
              <td class="tot">{w.pc.toFixed(1)}%</td>
              <td>{w.koreanSlots}</td>
              <td class="nm">{w.topTitle} — {w.topTitleSlots}, {w.topTitleShare.toFixed(1)}%</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      The last row is the lowest week in the record: across {data.countryCount} countries and
      {' '}{(data.countryCount * 20).toLocaleString('en-US')} published places, Korean titles held
      {' '}{data.troughWeeks[0].koreanSlots}. <b>The highest week in five years is four-fifths one
      show.</b> What moves the global figure is not a rising tide of Korean content — it is whether
      one enormous title happens to be out.
      {' '}<a href="/article/the-share-that-did-not-grow">The full story &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  {/*
    🔴 2026-08-08 22:3x — 「K드라마 성수기」를 잰 표. 달 폭은 2.2p 뿐이고 주 폭은 그 여섯 배다.
    ⛔ 「9월이 좋다」로 읽히게 두지 않는다. 오른쪽 칸(으뜸 한 편 뺀 값)을 **같은 표에** 둔다 —
       그 칸이 없으면 손님은 왼쪽 칸만 보고 철이 있다고 읽는다.
  */}
  <section>
    <h2>Is there a season?</h2>
    <p class="note">{season.question}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Month</th><th>Korean share</th>
            <th>Without that month&rsquo;s biggest title</th><th>That title</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {season.months.map((m) => (
            <tr class={m.pc === season.highest.pc || m.pc === season.lowest.pc ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{m.name}</td>
              <td class="tot">{m.pc}%</td>
              <td>{m.pcWithoutTop}%</td>
              <td class="nm"><i>{m.topTitle}</i> <span class="pc">{m.topTitleShareOfKorean}% of the month</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>The whole monthly range is {season.monthRange} points</b> — {season.lowest.pc}% in
      {' '}{season.lowest.name} to {season.highest.pc}% in {season.highest.name}, against
      {' '}{season.yearPc}% for the year. Remove each month&rsquo;s own biggest title and the band
      becomes <b>{season.monthRangeWithoutTop}</b>, so {season.verdict}.
      {' '}<i>{season.mostFrequentTopTitle.title}</i> is the biggest Korean title in
      {' '}{season.mostFrequentTopTitle.months} of the twelve months.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Set that against the weekly figures above: {season.weekLow.pc}% to {season.weekHigh.pc}% is a
      range of <b>{season.weekRange} points, {season.timesWeekBeatsMonth} times the monthly one</b>.
      The calendar is not what moves this number.
      {' '}<a href="/article/there-is-no-k-drama-season">The full story &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  {/*
    🔴 2026-08-09 14:0x — 8번이 자기 자에서 「조용히 **들어온** 것」을 찾은 것을 보고 내 것을 쟀다.
       우리는 「언어 딱지가 없는 작품 197편」을 적어 왔다. **작품 수만** 적었다.
       그 197편이 우리가 파는 수의 얼마를 떠받치는지는 한 번도 안 적었다 — **자리의 16.1%** 다.
    ⛔ 편수로는 작아 보이고 자리로는 안 작다. 그래서 자리로 낸다.
  */}
  <section>
    <h2>How much of this figure is confirmed, and how much is only unrefuted</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      A title enters this count because its name matches a Korean work in Wikidata. The rule that
      keeps foreign works of the same name out is Netflix's own <b>Non-English</b> label — but that
      label exists only on the global charts. A title that never reached a global top 10 has nothing
      to check against, so we <b>keep</b> it. That is not the same as confirming it.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Of the {data.koreanSlots.toLocaleString('en-US')} Korean chart places</th><th>Places</th><th>Share</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Carry Netflix's Non-English label — checked</td>
            <td>{data.confirmation.labelledSlots.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.confirmation.labelledPc}%</td></tr>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">No label at all — kept because we could not check</td>
            <td>{data.confirmation.unlabelledSlots.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.confirmation.unlabelledPc}%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Label says both — the name is on an English chart too</td>
            <td>{data.confirmation.bothSlots.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.confirmation.bothPc}%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>We had published the title count for years and never this one.</b> Saying "{reach.unlabelledTitles} titles carry
      no label" sounds like a rounding error; saying {data.confirmation.unlabelledPc}% of the places
      behind our headline figure rest on titles we could not check does not. Both are true, and the
      second is the one that matters when you are deciding whether to use the number.
    </p>
    <p class="note">{data.confirmation.note}</p>
    {data.confirmation.unkeyedSlots !== null && (
      <>
        <p class="note lead-in">
          <b>A second, independent check.</b> The language label asks whether a title is
          <i>not English</i>. It does not ask <i>which work this is</i>. For that we hold a Wikidata
          item number, matched by number rather than by name — and
          {' '}<b>{data.confirmation.keyedPc}%</b> of the places above sit on titles that have one.
          The remaining <b>{data.confirmation.unkeyedSlots} places ({data.confirmation.unkeyedPc}%)</b>
          {' '}entered on a name match alone.
        </p>
        <p class="note">
          The two checks catch different things and neither is a superset of the other, which is why
          both are printed. A title can carry a Wikidata number and still have no language label, and
          the reverse happens too.
        </p>
      </>
    )}
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/world-share" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li>
        <b>Every market enters on the same terms.</b> All {data.countryCount} publish exactly
        {' '}{data.slotsPerCountry.toLocaleString('en-US')} rows over the same {data.weekCount} weeks.
        The collector refuses to run if any two differ, because a country with a shorter record would
        otherwise look different for a reason that is not about Korean titles.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>One market is excluded and we name it.</b> {data.excludedCountry.name} —
        {' '}{data.excludedCountry.rows} rows over {data.excludedCountry.weeks} weeks, ending
        {' '}{data.excludedCountry.lastWeek}. {data.excludedCountry.why}
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Our identification, not Netflix's.</b> Netflix never states a title's country of origin on
        these lists. Korean titles here are identified through Wikidata, so the absolute level carries
        that method's error. The same ruler is applied to all {data.countryCount} markets, which is
        what makes the comparison between them, and across years, the firmer part.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Chart entry, not viewing.</b> Country lists carry a rank and no hours figure. Nothing here
        says how much anything was watched.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>No cause.</b> Netflix's own commissioning, the growth of local production, and more titles
        competing for the same twenty places are all plausible. This data separates none of them, and
        we have not gone looking for a story to fit.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <p class="back"><a href="/">&larr; Back to the front page</a></p>
</WikiTip>

<style>
  h1 { margin: 0 0 .5rem; font-size: 1.7rem; letter-spacing: -.02em; }
  .lead { margin: 0 0 .6rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .q { margin: 0 0 2rem; font-size: .9rem; color: var(--accent); }
  section { margin: 0 0 2.5rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin: 0 0 .7rem; letter-spacing: -.01em; }
  .note { font-size: .82rem; color: var(--muted); margin: .9rem 0 0; line-height: 1.6; }
  .note b { color: var(--ink); }

  /* 못 보는 것·의심스러운 것을 눈에 띄게 둔다 — 아래로 밀면 안 읽는다 */
  .warn {
    font-size: .86rem; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding: .8rem .9rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--card); border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
  }
  .warn b { color: var(--ink); }

  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .85rem; }
  th, td { padding: .45rem .55rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); text-align: right;
           white-space: nowrap; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  th { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .03em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
       font-weight: 700; }
  th:first-child, td.nm { text-align: left; white-space: normal; }
  td.tot { font-weight: 700; }
  td.sub { color: var(--muted); font-size: .78rem; text-align: left; white-space: normal; }
  td.names { font-size: .78rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.5; }
  tr.edge td { background: var(--card); }
  /* 폰에서 다섯 칸이 화면 밖으로 나가지 않게 — 3번이 8/8 에 그 자리에서 걸렸다 */
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    table { font-size: .78rem; }
    th, td { padding: .38rem .28rem; }
    td.names { font-size: .72rem; }
  }

  .src ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; font-size: .85rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; }
  .src li { margin: .45rem 0; }
  .src b { color: var(--ink); }
  .back { margin-top: 2.5rem; font-size: .9rem; }
</style>
